  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>Most renters do not think much about insurance until something goes wrong. Then a kitchen fire, a burst pipe in January, or a summer storm that sends water through a window suddenly makes it the most important thing in your life. Knowing how renters insurance works and what to do in the first few days after damage can make a stressful situation more manageable and help you recover what your policy actually covers.</p>
<p>This guide covers renters insurance basics, step-by-step actions after fire, water, mold, or storm damage, how to document losses, and how licensed public adjusters fit in for large claims.</p>

<h2>Renters Insurance Basics</h2>
<p>A typical renters insurance policy includes a few main types of coverage. Exact terms vary, so always read your own policy.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Personal property:</strong> Covers your belongings, like furniture, clothing, and electronics, against covered perils such as fire, smoke, theft, and certain kinds of water damage.</li>
<li><strong>Liability:</strong> Helps if you are found responsible for injuring someone or damaging someone else's property, including damage to your unit or a neighbor's.</li>
<li><strong>Additional living expenses (loss of use):</strong> Helps pay for a hotel, meals, and other extra costs if a covered event makes your apartment unlivable.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Replacement cost vs. actual cash value</h3>
<p>This is one of the most important details in your policy. Actual cash value pays what your items were worth at the time of loss, after depreciation. Replacement cost pays what it costs to buy a comparable new item. A five-year-old couch might be worth very little under actual cash value but enough to buy a new one under replacement cost.</p>
<h3>What is commonly excluded</h3>
<p>Standard renters policies often exclude flood damage from rising water, earthquakes, and damage from gradual leaks or neglect. Mold coverage is frequently limited. Expensive jewelry, collectibles, and electronics may have sub-limits unless you add extra coverage. Check with your agent if any of these concern you.</p>
<p>And remember: your landlord's insurance generally covers the building, not your belongings. If your lease requires renters insurance, it is protecting you as much as the property. Budget for it alongside rent; our <a href="/blog/apartment-budget-guide/">apartment budget guide</a> can help.</p>

<h2>What to Do Right After Damage</h2>
<h3>1. Get safe first</h3>
<p>If there is a fire, gas smell, or electrical hazard, leave and call 911. Do not go back in until the fire department or your landlord says it is safe.</p>
<h3>2. Notify your landlord</h3>
<p>Report the damage to your property manager as soon as possible, by phone for emergencies and in writing for a record. Your landlord is generally responsible for repairs to the building itself. Our <a href="/blog/renter-maintenance-checklist/">renter maintenance checklist</a> explains what to report and when.</p>
<h3>3. Prevent further damage</h3>
<p>Most policies expect you to take reasonable steps to prevent additional damage. That can mean moving belongings away from water, shutting off a valve if you know how, or covering items with plastic. Keep receipts for anything you buy to protect your property.</p>
<h3>4. Contact your insurer promptly</h3>
<p>Call your insurance company or agent, or file online, as soon as you can. Ask about the claim process, deadlines, and whether you are eligible for additional living expenses if you cannot stay in your apartment.</p>

<!--products-->

<h2>Specific Situations</h2>
<h3>Fire and smoke</h3>
<p>Even a small fire can cause smoke and soot damage throughout an apartment, affecting clothing, upholstery, and electronics far from the flames. Do not throw away damaged items until your insurer has documented them or told you it is okay. Smoke odor can linger, so ask about professional cleaning coverage.</p>
<h3>Water damage and mold</h3>
<p>Sudden water damage, like a burst pipe or an overflowing upstairs unit, is commonly covered, while gradual leaks often are not. Act quickly: wet materials can begin growing mold within a day or two. Report leaks right away, photograph everything, and dry what you can. If mold appears, notify your landlord in writing, since the building materials are their responsibility. Ohio winters make frozen pipes a real risk, so keep the heat on even when you travel.</p>
<h3>Storm damage</h3>
<p>Severe thunderstorms and high winds happen across the Miami Valley. Wind-driven rain through a broken window or roof damage is often covered, but flooding from rising water typically is not covered by a standard renters policy. If you live near the Great Miami River or another waterway, ask your agent whether separate flood insurance makes sense for you.</p>

<h2>Documenting Your Losses</h2>
<p>Good documentation is the single biggest factor in a smooth claim.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Make a home inventory now.</strong> Walk through your apartment with your phone and record a video of every room, opening closets and drawers. Store it in the cloud or on an external drive.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph the damage</strong> before you clean up or move anything, from several angles.</li>
<li><strong>List every damaged item</strong> with a description, approximate age, where you bought it, and what it cost if you know.</li>
<li><strong>Gather receipts</strong> from email, bank statements, or online order history.</li>
<li><strong>Keep a claim log</strong> with dates, names, and notes from every conversation with your insurer and landlord.</li>
<li><strong>Save expense receipts</strong> for hotels, meals, and emergency purchases.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How Licensed Public Adjusters Can Help</h2>
<p>When you file a claim, your insurance company assigns its own adjuster to evaluate the loss on the insurer's behalf. A public adjuster is different: they are licensed professionals hired by the policyholder to prepare, document, and negotiate the claim on the policyholder's behalf. In Ohio, public adjusters must be licensed through the Ohio Department of Insurance.</p>
<h3>When a public adjuster might be worth considering</h3>
<ul>
<li>A large loss, such as a fire that destroys most of your belongings</li>
<li>A complex claim involving many items, smoke damage, or additional living expenses</li>
<li>A dispute with the insurer about what is covered or how much it is worth</li>
<li>Property managers dealing with building-wide damage under a commercial policy</li>
</ul>
<h3>Things to know before hiring one</h3>
<ul>
<li>Public adjusters are typically paid a percentage of the settlement, so their fee reduces your payout. For a small claim, it may not be worth it.</li>
<li>Verify the adjuster's license with the Ohio Department of Insurance.</li>
<li>Read the contract carefully, including the fee, cancellation terms, and what services are included.</li>
<li>Be cautious of anyone who pressures you to sign immediately after a disaster.</li>
</ul>
<p>Many renters handle their claims successfully on their own. A public adjuster is one option among several, along with working directly with your insurer, asking your agent for help, or consulting an attorney in a serious dispute. This is general information, not legal or insurance advice.</p>

<h2>Be Prepared Before Anything Happens</h2>
<p>Review your policy once a year, keep your inventory current, store important documents in a fireproof bag, and know your landlord's emergency maintenance number. If you are moving soon, add renters insurance to your <a href="/tools/moving-checklist/">moving checklist</a> so coverage starts the day you get your keys. A little preparation now makes a hard day much easier to recover from.</p>
